(a) Emergency suspension.
(1) The chief of police shall notify the mayor that such a situation exists at a licensed/permitted establishment or event that constitutes a significant, present threat to public safety or public order, which is likely to continue and that requires the immediate suspension of a license;
(2) A decision to immediately suspend a license or permit shall be jointly made by the chief of police and the mayor and shall be justified when any of the criteria set forth in subsection 4-32(b)(12) is present and at least one identifiable aggravating circumstance exists that the mayor finds requires an emergency suspension, after consideration of the following:
a. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set.
b. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
c. Impact of the offense on the community;
d. History of prior suspensions and/or revocations of licensee and of warnings of violations to licensee or permit holder;
e. Disorderly conduct at the establishment or event;
f. Presence of illegal drugs or weapons at the establishment or event;
g. Violence at the establishment or event;
h. Gambling at the establishment or event;
i. Any public safety implications of failing to immediately suspend the subject license or permit; and
j. Whether the situation in question is a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
(3) At least within 24 hours of said decision, the mayor shall issue a show cause order notifying the licensee/permit holder of the decision to immediately suspend said license/permit; notifying licensee/permit holder of a time, date, and place of the review hearing to be conducted before the mayor no later than 20 days from the date of the emergency suspension; and directing the licensee/permit holder to show cause why said license/permit should not be subject to further suspension, revocation or a supervised action plan; both licensee/permit holder and the chief of police shall receive a copy of the show cause order;
(4) The city clerk or designee shall be authorized to issue no more than three subpoenas on behalf of each party regarding witnesses for said review hearing, unless further subpoenas are approved in writing by the mayor;
(5) At said review hearing, the licensee/permit holder shall be allowed to present evidence to the mayor to show cause why the license or permit should not be further suspended, revoked or subject to a supervised action plan, and the chief of police or his/her designee shall be allowed to present evidence to the mayor as to why the license/permit should be further suspended, revoked or subject to a supervised action plan;
(6) Strict evidentiary rules shall not apply to said review hearing;
(7) After consideration of all evidence presented at the emergency review hearing, the mayor shall issue his/her decision to either:
a. Mandate a supervised action plan;
b. Further suspend the subject license/permit for a certain time period; and/or
c. Revoke the subject license/permit.
(8) Within 72 hours of the conclusion of the review hearing, the mayor shall provide written notice of his/her decision to the licensee/permit holder and the chief of police. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify either party of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter. A review of the mayor's decision may be made by appeal to the city council on the record only; no further evidentiary hearings shall be held before the city council. Such appeal shall be by written petition, filed in the office of the city clerk or designee within 15 days after entry of the mayor's final decision. It shall be the duty of the city clerk or designee to forward a copy of said appeal and the record to the city council. In order to defray administrative costs, said written petition must be accompanied by a filing fee of $500.00 from licensee/permit holder.
(9) Appeal to city council.
a. The filing of an appeal after an emergency suspension process shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the mayor, so states in the written decision.
b. The city council may affirm, reverse, or modify the findings of the mayor.
c. The findings shall be affirmed unless found to be:
1. Contrary to law or ordinances; or
2. There is no evidence in the record to support the findings of the mayor, provided, however, that the city council may modify the findings as set forth herein.
d. In the event of a modification of the findings of the mayor, the city council shall consider the following factors:
1. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set;
2. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
3. Impact of the offense on the community;
4. History of prior suspensions and/or revocations of licensee/permit holder and of warnings of violations to licensee/permit holder;
5. Disorderly conduct at the establishment or event;
6. Presence of illegal drugs or weapons at the establishment or event;
7. Violence at the establishment or event;
8. Gambling at the establishment or event;
9. Any public safety implications of failing to immediately suspend the subject license; and
10. Whether the situation in question is a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
(10) The findings of the city council shall be final unless appealed within 30 days of the date of said findings by certiorari to the superior court of the county. The filing of an appeal after an emergency hearing process shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the city council so states in its decision.
(b) Other hearings.
(1) Except as set forth in this chapter, no license or permit shall be suspended or revoked without the opportunity for a hearing as hereinafter provided. The mayor shall hold evidentiary hearings regarding the imposition of supervised action plans, suspensions and/or revocations of all licenses or permits and prior to finalizing a denial of an application for a permit.
(2) In the event the mayor intends to deny a permit application or mandate a supervised action plan, suspend or revoke a license, s/he shall provide written notice to the applicant/licensee/permit holder that applicant/licensee/permit holder shall show cause before the mayor why said license/permit should not be denied, subject to a supervised action plan, suspended or revoked; said order shall notify applicant/licensee/permit holder and the chief of police of the time, date, and place of the hearing to be conducted before the mayor;
(3) The city clerk or designee shall be authorized to issue no more than three subpoenas on behalf of each party regarding witnesses for said hearing, unless further subpoenas are approved in writing by the mayor;
(4) At said hearing, applicant/licensee/permit holder shall be allowed to present evidence to the mayor to show cause why the application should not be denied, or the license/permit subjected to a supervised action plan, suspended or revoked, and if necessary, the chief of police or his/her designee shall be allowed to present evidence to the mayor as to why the application should be denied or the license/permit subjected to a supervised action plan, suspended or revoked;
(5) Strict evidentiary rules shall not apply to said hearing;
(6) After consideration of all evidence presented at the hearing, the mayor shall issue his/her decision to either:
a. Mandate a supervised action plan;
b. Suspend the subject license for a certain time period;
c. Revoke the subject license; and/or
d. Deny an application.
(7) After said hearing, the mayor shall provide written notice to applicant/licensee/permit holder and the chief of police of his/her decision. Such written notification shall set forth in reasonable detail the reasons for such action and shall notify either party of the right to appeal under the provisions of this chapter. A review of the decision of the mayor may be made by appeal to the city council on the record only; no further evidentiary hearings shall be held before the city council. Such appeal shall be by written petition, filed in the office of the city clerk or designee within 15 days after entry of the final decision of the mayor. It shall be the duty of the city clerk or designee to forward a copy of said appeal and the record to the city council. In order to defray administrative costs, said written petition must be accompanied by a filing fee of $500.00 from applicant/licensee/permit holder.
(8) Appeal to city council.
a. Except for emergency suspensions, the filing of such appeal to the city council shall act as a supersedeas until such time as the city council votes on the appeal or for a period of 45 days, whichever period of time is shorter.
b. The city council may affirm, reverse, or modify the findings of the mayor.
c. The findings of the mayor shall be affirmed unless found to be:
1. Contrary to law or ordinances, or
2. There is no evidence in the record to support the findings of the mayor, provided, however, that the city council may modify the findings as set forth herein.
d. In the event of a modification of the findings of the mayor, the city council shall consider the following factors:
1. Consistency of penalties mandated by this chapter and those previously set;
2. Likelihood of deterring future wrongdoing;
3. Impact of the offense on the community;
4. Any mitigating or aggravating circumstances;
5. Any remedial or corrective steps taken by licensee/permit holder or the failure by licensee/permit holder to take remedial or corrective steps; and
6. Whether the situation involves a violation of a supervised action plan and/or suspension previously imposed.
(9) The findings of the city council shall be final unless appealed within 30 days of the date of said findings by certiorari to the superior court of the county. The filing of such an appeal shall not act as a supersedeas, unless the city council so states in its decision.
(c) The requirement of service of any written decision, communication, notice, and/or supervised action plan to a licensee shall be deemed satisfied with hand-delivery of said written document to the physical location of the licensed/permitted establishment and given to any employee, contractor, and or representative of the licensee/permit holder, unless the licensee/permit holder requests in writing service by some other reasonable means and delivers same to the city at the time of the initial granting of the license/permit and/or at each renewal of the license. Any other notice, including, but not limited to hearing notices and a denial of an application, shall be deemed delivered when personally served or by mail, within three days after the date of deposit in the United States mail.
(d) Except for emergency suspensions, in the event an applicant/licensee/permit holder wishes to request a continuance of any hearing and/or an appeal, the request must be in writing and must be delivered no later than seven days prior to the hearing or appeal. The mayor is authorized to deny or grant said request for good cause shown.
(Ord. No. 2020-09-01, § 1(Exh. A), 12-1-2020)

